"The revelation of Surah al-Fath is narrated in a hadith transmitted from Umar: While on an expedition with the Prophet (peace be upon him), he asked a question three times, and when he received no answer, he grew concerned. Then he heard a voice, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to him: 'Tonight a surah was revealed to me that is dearer to me than the world and all that is in it: \"Truly, We have granted you a clear victory.\"' This surah was revealed after the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah, somewhere between Mecca and Medina.\n\nThe surah has 29 verses, 560 words, and 2,430 letters. Its rhyme-letter (the common final letter of the verses) is 'nun'. Its connection to the preceding surah, Surah Muhammad, is clear: in the former, repentance and seeking forgiveness were commanded, while here forgiveness is proclaimed; that one concluded with a warning of replacement, whereas this one begins with the glad tidings of victory.\n\nThe word 'victory' in verse 1 refers, according to most commentators, to the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah. This treaty ensured the recognition of the Muslims as a state and opened the door to subsequent conquests. The purpose of the Prophet (peace be upon him) in this expedition was not war but umrah; however, upon encountering the polytheists, peace was made, and this became an instrument for the spread of Islam.\n\nIn verses 2–3, the wisdom behind the victory is explained in four points: Allah forgiving the past and future sins of the Prophet, completing His favor upon him, guiding him to a straight path, and aiding him with a mighty victory. Here, according to most commentators, the expression 'sin' refers to instances such as the Prophet occasionally choosing the less optimal alternative in his independent judgment (ijtihad); furthermore, this forgiveness also signifies the easing of the hardships encountered in the Prophet's mission of messengership.\n\nIn verse 4, the concept of 'tranquility' (sakinah) is addressed. Tranquility is the inner peace and reassurance that descends upon the heart; it is sent down into the hearts of the believers so that they may add faith to their faith. This tranquility is one of the hosts of Allah, and by bringing peace to the believers, it prepares them for great victories. Allah is the Master of the hosts of the heavens and the earth; He knows all things and governs with wisdom.\n\nIn verses 5–7, two more reasons of wisdom behind this victory are mentioned: admitting believing men and women into gardens and overlooking their misdeeds, and subjecting to punishment the hypocrites and polytheists who harbor evil thoughts about Allah. This punishment is the consequence of their evil thoughts. To Allah belong the hosts of the heavens and the earth; He is Almighty and All-Wise.\n\nIn verses 8–10, the duty of the Prophet is explained as a witness, a bearer of glad tidings, and a warner. The believers must believe in Allah and His Messenger, honor and support Him, and glorify Him morning and evening. Furthermore, the pledge of allegiance (bay'ah) made to the Prophet was in reality made to Allah; whoever breaks this pledge breaks it only to his own detriment, while whoever fulfills it will receive an immense reward.\n\nVerses 11–14 describe the excuses and true nature of the Bedouins who did not join the expedition of Hudaybiyyah. They will say, 'Our wealth and our families kept us occupied, so ask forgiveness for us.' Yet they say with their tongues what is not in their hearts. Allah informs them that they assumed the Prophet and the believers would never return, and for this reason they deserved destruction. For whoever does not believe in Allah and His Messenger, Hell has been prepared.\n\nIn verses 15–17, those who stayed behind from the battle are criticized for wanting to join only for the spoils of war and attempting to alter the word of Allah. They are informed that they will be called to fight against a people of great might: if they obey, there will be a fair reward, but if not, a painful punishment. Those with valid excuses (the blind, the lame, and the sick) are not held accountable; whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger will enter Paradise, while those who turn away will suffer punishment.\n\nThe Bedouins who stayed behind from the battle will say, when you set out to take the spoils, 'Allow us to follow you.' They wish to change the word of Allah. Allah has promised the spoils to those who were present at Hudaybiyyah. Say to them: 'You shall never follow us. Thus has Allah declared beforehand.' Then they will say, 'No, you envy us.' In truth, they understand little of the reality.\n\nSay to those Bedouins who remained behind: 'You will be called to fight against a people of great might.' It has been narrated that this people refers to Banu Hanifah, the Persians, or the Byzantines. These opinions are given by way of example, not limitation.\n\nThere is no blame upon the blind, the lame, or the sick for not going to war. They have valid excuses. If they wish and are able to go, that is also permissible. Indeed, Ibn Umm Maktum took part in the Battle of al-Qadisiyyah despite being blind.\n\nCertainly, Allah was well pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you under the tree at Hudaybiyyah. He knew what was in their hearts, sent down reassurance upon them, and rewarded them with a near victory (Khaybar). He also promised them abundant spoils of war. Allah has promised you abundant spoils that you will capture, granted this to you promptly, and restrained the hands of the people from you.\n\nIf the disbelievers had fought against you, they would surely have turned their backs and fled. Such is the established way of Allah, and you will find no change in His way. It is He who held back your hands after making you victorious over them in the heart of Mecca. For there were believing men and women there whom you did not yet know; Allah prevented fighting so that you would not trample them. Had they been separated, We would surely have subjected those who disbelieved to a painful punishment.\n\nThose who disbelieved had harbored in their hearts the fanatical zeal of the pre-Islamic days of ignorance (jahiliyyah). Thereupon Allah sent down His tranquility and reassurance upon His Messenger and the believers, and bound them to the word of piety (taqwa), for they were well entitled to it and worthy of it. And Allah is Knower of all things.\n\nUmar was troubled by the peace treaty and objected to the Prophet (peace be upon him), but Abu Bakr calmed him, saying that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was upon the truth and that his promise would certainly be fulfilled. Against the pagan zeal of ignorance displayed by the polytheists, Allah Almighty sent down His tranquility upon His Messenger and the believers, soothing them and keeping them firm upon the word of piety (taqwa).\n\nThere are differing views regarding the word of piety (kalimat al-taqwa): according to some, it is the word of monotheism (tawhid) and the declaration of faith (shahadah); according to others, it is loyalty to the peace treaty and fidelity to the pledge. Because the believers were more deserving and worthy of this word than anyone else, Allah kept them steadfast upon it.\n\nAllah has fulfilled the vision of His Prophet in truth. The believers will enter the Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Haram) in security, with their heads shaved or hair shortened. However, this will occur when Allah wills. The condition 'if Allah wills' (insha'Allah) indicates that the fulfillment of the promise depends on the will of Allah and that there may be those among them who will pass away before then. Prior to this, Allah granted an imminent victory, such as the peace of Hudaybiyyah and the conquest of Khaybar.\n\nAllah has sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth. This religion shall prevail over all other religions. This superiority is both in terms of evidence and knowledge, as well as actual victory. And Allah is sufficient as a witness.\n\nMuhammad (peace be upon him) is the Messenger of Allah. Those who are with him are firm against the disbelievers and compassionate among themselves. They are constantly bowing and prostrating, seeking bounty and good pleasure from Allah. On their faces are marks from the traces of prostration. Their description is likewise given in the Torah and the Gospel: they are like a seed that puts forth its shoot, strengthens it, and delights the sowers. In this manner, Allah increases and strengthens the believers, enraging the disbelievers through them. To those who believe and do righteous deeds, He has promised forgiveness and an immense reward."